# Contributing to AïkiGrade

Thanks for your interest in AïkiGrade! This app helps Aïkido practitioners prepare for their grade exams, and every contribution, from a typo fix to a new feature, is welcome.

## Table of contents

- [Ways to contribute](#ways-to-contribute)
- [Getting started](#getting-started)
- [Project structure](#project-structure)
- [Development workflow](#development-workflow)
- [Coding guidelines](#coding-guidelines)
- [Contributing technique content](#contributing-technique-content)
- [Roadmap](#roadmap)
- [Questions](#questions)

## Ways to contribute

- **Report a bug**: open an [issue](https://github.com/LouisLeNezet/AikiGrade/issues) with the steps to reproduce, what you expected, what happened, and your platform (browser or Android version).
- **Suggest an improvement**: open an issue describing the need first, then the idea.
- **Fix or complete technique data**: corrections to the technique list, descriptions or video links are very valuable (see [Contributing technique content](#contributing-technique-content)).
- **Improve the documentation**: README, screenshots, translations.
- **Write code**: pick an item from the [Roadmap](#roadmap) or an issue labelled `good first issue`.

If you plan a large change, please open an issue first so we can agree on the approach before you invest time.

## Getting started

### Prerequisites

- [Flutter SDK](https://docs.flutter.dev/get-started/install) 3.32 or later (check `pubspec.yaml` for the exact constraint)
- Dart 3.8 or later (bundled with Flutter)
- Chrome, to run the web version
- Android Studio or an Android device/emulator, to run the Android version

Check your setup with:

```bash
flutter doctor
```

### Run the app locally

```bash
# 1. Fork the repository on GitHub, then clone your fork
git clone https://github.com/<your-username>/AikiGrade.git
cd AikiGrade

# 2. Install dependencies
flutter pub get

# 3. Run in the browser
flutter run -d chrome

# Or on a connected Android device / emulator
flutter run -d android
```

### Run the checks

```bash
flutter analyze   # static analysis (lints)
flutter test      # unit and widget tests
```

Both must pass before you open a pull request.

## Project structure

| Path | Content |
| --- | --- |
| `lib/` | Application source code (Dart) |
| `test/` | Unit and widget tests |
| `assets/csv/` | Technique data and exams setup |
| `assets/markdowns/` | Technique descriptions |
| `assets/config.json` | Application configuration |
| `assets/images/` | Images and logos |
| `web/`, `android/` | Platform-specific code |

## Development workflow

1. **Fork** the repository and create a branch from `dev`:
   ```bash
   git checkout dev
   git pull
   git checkout -b feature/short-description
   ```
   Use a prefix such as `feature/`, `fix/`, `docs/` or `chore/`.
2. **Make your changes**, in small and focused commits.
3. **Add or update tests** when you change behaviour, especially anything touching saved data.
4. **Run** `flutter analyze` and `flutter test`.
5. **Push** your branch and open a **pull request against `dev`**.
6. In the pull request, explain *what* changed and *why*, link the related issue (`Closes #12`), and add screenshots for any UI change.

### Commit messages

Write short, imperative messages, for example:

```
Add JSON export of user progress
Fix crash when loading empty history
```

## Coding guidelines

- Follow the [Dart style guide](https://dart.dev/effective-dart/style) and format your code with `dart format .`.
- Fix all warnings reported by `flutter analyze`.
- Keep UI, state and data-access logic separated so they can be tested independently.
- **Never break users' saved progress.** Data is stored locally and there is no cloud backup. Any change to the saved JSON format must keep old data readable (see the schema versioning item in the roadmap).
- Keep the privacy stance of the project: no account system, no tracking, no cloud.
- Avoid adding a new dependency unless it is clearly worth it, and mention it in your pull request.

## Contributing technique content

The techniques follow the FFAAA handbook.

- Technique lists are in `assets/csv/`, descriptions in `assets/markdowns/`.
- Do **not** change the identifier of an existing technique: user progress is linked to it. Fix names or descriptions freely, but keep the ID.
- For a new technique, add its row to the CSV and a matching markdown file, then check it displays correctly in the app.

## Roadmap

Contributions on any of these items are welcome. Items are grouped by theme; open an issue before starting so work is not duplicated.

### Data safety (priority)

- [ ] Stable technique IDs, so renaming or correcting a technique never breaks saved progress
- [ ] Schema version in the saved JSON, with a small migration path so updates never wipe users' progress
- [ ] Export (and import) of progress as JSON, so users can keep and move their data

### Features

- [ ] Spaced repetition: surface techniques the user rates poorly or has not reviewed recently, instead of only letting them pick
- [ ] Offline support (PWA for the web version)
- [ ] Multilingual support (French and English first)
- [ ] Dark mode

### Tooling and releases

- [x] GitHub Actions workflow running `flutter analyze` and `flutter test` on every push and pull request, with a status badge in the README
- [x] GitHub Actions workflow building and deploying the web version to GitHub Pages
- [ ] GitHub Actions workflow building an Android APK when a version tag is pushed
- [x] Remove unused platform folders and keep only `web` and `android`
- [ ] First tagged release (`v0.1.0`)

## Questions

If something is unclear, open an issue or start a discussion on the repository. AïkiGrade is developed by the Aïkido club of Le Rheu, France, and we are happy to help newcomers get started.

By contributing, you agree that your contributions will be released under the project's license.
